// C11 6.7: an init-declarator list is separated by `,`. After an initializer
// the declaration reader inferred "the engine already ate the `,`" from the
// token that followed, so `int y = a b;` began a second declarator `b`
// ("redefinition of 'b'"). Only a consumed `,` continues the list now.
// Oracle: gcc "error: expected ',' or ';' before 'b'", clang "error: expected
// ';' at end of declaration".
int main(void) { int a = 1, b = 2; int y = a b; return y; }
